# Locus Root .gitignore
#
# NOTE: gitignore has no inline comments. A `#` only starts a comment at the
# BEGINNING of a line — anywhere else it is part of the pattern. Two secret
# files were "ignored" here with trailing comments and were therefore NOT
# ignored at all, and ended up committed with a live tier password in them
# (removed in FIXES.md 34). Never put a comment on the same line as a pattern.

# Generated code files
*-codes.txt
*-cards.pdf

# Build artifacts
dist/
build/bin/
frontend/dist/
node_modules/
*.exe
*.zip
*.tar.gz

# Rust/Tauri client build output. `client/` has its own .gitignore covering
# these; repeated here so a root-level `git add -A` cannot pick them up.
client/target/
# NOTE: the sidecar directory is excluded by CONTENTS (`sidecar/*`), not
# wholesale. Git cannot re-include a file inside an excluded directory, so a
# directory-level pattern here would silently defeat the vendored-alpha
# exception documented below. That is not a hypothetical: the first version of
# this change used `client/src-tauri/sidecar/` and the alpha stayed ignored.
client/src-tauri/sidecar/*
#
# EXCEPTION: the alpha engine is VENDORED, deliberately.
#
# Every other sidecar is a reproducible download from a stable release URL, so
# ignoring it costs nothing. The mihomo ALPHA is published as a single rolling
# prerelease — MetaCubeX deletes the previous alpha the moment a new one lands —
# so its download URL stops existing. On 2026-10-26 that deleted the only copy
# of `alpha-94b13ae` anywhere, and the client could not compile: `tauri.conf.json`
# lists the alpha in `externalBin`, so the build requires a file that nothing
# could reproduce.
#
# A file the build requires and the network cannot supply has to live in the
# repository. The cost is ~62 MiB; the alternative is that no fresh clone can
# build the client. `prebuild.mjs` prefers this vendored copy and does not fetch
# it; `server/scripts/sidecar-lock.py` pins its hash like any other input.
#
# This un-ignores exactly this one path. It does NOT un-ignore the directory.
!client/src-tauri/sidecar/verge-mihomo-alpha-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu

# Signed release binaries and their manifest, staged here by publish-release.sh
# before upload. ~330 MB per release, reproducible from the tag, and never
# something a checkout needs — but git did not know that, and it showed up as
# untracked in every `git status`, one `git add -A` away from a permanent place
# in the history. Found 2026-10-02 while preparing a release; the same one-line
# mistake the vpspassword entry above records.
release-artifacts/
client/src-tauri/resources/
client/src-tauri/gen/schemas/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# OS junk
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo

# Windows "downloaded from the Internet" markers (Zone.Identifier, ZoneId=3).
#
# On NTFS these are invisible alternate data streams on the file they belong
# to. Copy the file to a filesystem that has no streams — or with a tool that
# materialises them — and they become real files named `photo.png:Zone.Identifier`.
# A colon is illegal in a Windows path component, so once one is committed Git
# for Windows refuses to check out ANY commit containing it:
#
#   error: invalid path '.../128x128.png:Zone.Identifier'
#
# That is exactly how the Windows CI job broke (FIXES.md, 2026-09-27). The
# pattern is anchored to the suffix so it cannot accidentally ignore a real
# file whose name merely contains a colon.
*:Zone.Identifier

# Diagnostic scratch (probe logs, one-off scripts)
diag/
whale.txt

# Local Go toolchain bootstrapped for builds on a host with no system Go.
# Never part of the repository; see docs for the bootstrap procedure.
.gotool/

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml

# Scratch npm packages. These were pulled into the repo root while building a
# one-off tool and got committed by `git add -A` — the same way node_modules
# would. Anything installed outside client/ or server/console/ is here by
# accident.
/tweetnacl/
/tweetnacl-util/

# Deploy key for pushing to the VPS from this machine. Not a signing key and
# not a secret anyone else needs — but it is still a credential, and one that
# would grant root shell on the hub if it leaked.
.deploy/

# Locus update signing keys. The private key signs every shipped client update;
# a leak lets anyone publish a build every installed client will accept, and a
# loss means no installed client can ever be updated again. Never commit either
# file — the public key belongs in tauri.conf.json, the private key in CI
# secrets and offline custody.
.locus-keys/

# Secrets (encrypted is fine; plaintext never)
.secrets.env
age-key.txt

# Clash Verge client profiles. These embed the tier's shadowsocks password in
# cleartext, so they must never be committed.
clash-verge-stealth.yaml
clash-verge-stealth-notun.yaml
clash-verge-*.yaml

# Obsidian vault config (personal notes tooling, not part of the project).
# Was partially tracked — only workspace.json was ignored — which is why the
# whole directory is ignored now. Files stay on disk; none are tracked.
.obsidian/

# Local agent-runner config (whale). Never part of the project.
.whale/

# The PRE-MIGRATION repository, moved alongside this one so its history (and the
# original signing key) can be read from it. It is a separate git repository —
# `git add -A` here would otherwise try to absorb it as a submodule/gitlink, and
# it carries the old commit history the migration deliberately did not bring
# across.
#
# Nothing in it is part of this project's source. Read from it; never build.
VPN-Service/

# Release worktrees. A tagged release is rebuilt from the historical commit it
# names, in a linked worktree, so the release does not depend on (or disturb)
# whatever is in the main working tree at the time. `git worktree add` places
# the linked worktree's metadata in the main repo's `.git/worktrees/`, and the
# checkout itself lives here. Nothing in it is source; it is a build directory
# that happens to be a git checkout.
release-worktrees/
.aider*
# A plaintext root credential for the VPS, kept in the working tree because it
# has to be usable from here. The pattern carries NO `./` prefix: gitignore
# matches relative to the .gitignore's own directory, so `./vpspassword.txt`
# matched nothing and `git add -A` staged the password (caught 2026-10-02, one
# command short of committing a live credential to a public-looking repo).
vpspassword.txt

# Recovery bundle: live credentials pulled back from the hub and the CI secret
# store (age private key, plaintext tier passwords, decrypted secrets). Kept in
# the working tree so the operator has them to hand, for the same reason as
# vpspassword.txt above — and with the same failure mode if this line is
# missing: `git add -A` stages them. Like vpspassword.txt the pattern carries
# NO `./` prefix. Never commit; never build from here.
.recovered/

# Temporary GitHub personal access token, generated to pull secrets back out of
# the Actions store and deleted again afterwards. Caught 2026-10-15 sitting
# untracked and unignored in the working tree — one `git add -A` from publishing
# an admin:org + delete_repo + repo token to a public repository. The name is a
# wildcard so an operator who numbers or suffixes the file is still covered.
temp-ghp-token*

# Passphrase for server/secrets.env.age — the file the deploy scripts decrypt.
#
# This is the highest-value file that can exist in this working tree. The
# ciphertext is COMMITTED ON PURPOSE, so the passphrase is the only thing
# between a public clone and every credential in the project (B2 keys,
# PocketBase admin, tier passwords, the admin API token). A leaked ciphertext
# alone is an offline-attack problem; a leaked ciphertext plus its passphrase is
# total compromise with no brute force required.
#
# Added 2026-10-16: `encryption-password.txt` was created at the repo ROOT
# during the migration, untracked and unignored — exactly the shape of the
# vpspassword.txt and temp-ghp-token incidents. Same failure mode, same fix.
#
# Wildcards, so a suffixed or numbered file is covered; and NO `./` prefix, for
# the reason recorded against vpspassword.txt above. server/.gitignore holds the
# same patterns for the server/ subtree.
encryption-password*
secrets-passphrase*
secrets-pass*
.passphrase
passphrase.txt
